🔢 STM32 — Display Digits 0 to 9 on LEDs (0–9 Counter Project)
📘 Project Overview

This project demonstrates how to display numbers 0 to 9 using 8 individual LEDs connected to GPIO Port B of the STM32 Nucleo-C031C6 board.
Each digit is created by turning specific LEDs ON, while turning the remaining LEDs OFF, forming a custom LED-pattern for each number.

The code continuously loops from 0 → 9, with a 1-second delay between each number.

🛠️ What This Project Teaches

✔ Understanding GPIO configuration in STM32CubeIDE
✔ Controlling multiple pins using HAL_GPIO_WritePin()
✔ Using bitwise OR (|) to group multiple pins
✔ Creating LED patterns using set/reset masks
✔ Using HAL_Delay() for time control

This project is perfect for beginners learning GPIO control.

💡 How It Works

Each number (0–9) is represented by two masks:

1️⃣ Set Mask (D0S, D1S, …)

LEDs that should turn ON to form the digit.

2️⃣ Reset Mask (D0R, D1R, …)

LEDs that should turn OFF for that digit.

Example:

HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOB, D0S, GPIO_PIN_SET);    // Turn ON required LEDs for 0
HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOB, D0R, GPIO_PIN_RESET);  // Turn OFF remaining LEDs
HAL_Delay(1000);                                // Wait 1 second


This is repeated for digits 0,1,2,…9.
